# CS193V exit message, for a login shell that is NOT a tmux tab.
#
# Installed as /home/student/.bash_logout, which bash runs when a LOGIN shell exits. The
# text lives in /usr/local/bin/cs193v-goodbye; this file only decides whether to run it.
#
# THE $TMUX GUARD. tmux runs a login shell in every tab, so without it this fires once per
# TAB -- a farewell printed into a pane that is in the act of closing, for something the
# student has not actually left. Worse, it would say "you have left the CS193V development
# environment" while they are still sitting in it with four other tabs open.
#
# On the tmux path the goodbye is printed by cs193v-shell instead, after the whole session
# ends and tmux has restored the host terminal from the alternate screen. What is left for
# this file is `podman exec -it cs193v bash -l`, the documented path for scripts and for
# diagnosis, which has no tmux around it and should still say goodbye.
#
# It will not run if the shell is killed rather than exited. That is acceptable because
# this is cosmetic -- nothing here needs undoing. It is exactly why the rejected
# background-colour idea would have had to live in the launcher instead: a colour left
# applied after an unclean exit would follow a student into unrelated work.

case $- in
    *i*)
        if [ -t 1 ] && [ -z "${TMUX:-}" ]; then
            cs193v-goodbye
        fi
        ;;
esac
